:answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436827} {:answer/id #uuid "a770a7ff-df6f-5dec-bec7-267dbaa53948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436837} {:answer/id #uuid "e3516f72-8edb-54cf-a1ba-115368557ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca56ef0-f2a8-56e7-a0f1-0c84b89b4f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436824} :answer-field/id #uuid "827eac35-448a-5c6d-a1b3-05ca38d592f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77263833-2d61-5397-981f-0a043abf2225" :question/math-academy-id "q-73451" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-5,2,3⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨1,-5,4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-5,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64225d0e-e8e7-5a62-ba67-a47c2125c90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436832} {:answer/id #uuid "a490d505-52ab-5416-8a32-5429f938b745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436835} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ebf10e-c933-5ba3-a9e0-1a62a27771b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436838} {:answer/id #uuid "f98f5e82-18cb-5ae0-8ec5-0ba8c856e8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436845} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e0cc5c-df06-54c7-9fce-ac55f53eeb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64225d0e-e8e7-5a62-ba67-a47c2125c90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436832} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e1a389-e227-565c-848f-f4e0c19bfd02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97c82ac3-b53e-55cf-b9b8-2b725636dcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-128093" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨5,-8,-7⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,1,2⟩,$ and $c=⟨-3,1,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨5,-8,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-6)+(-7)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27685b8e-75cd-51d4-8f57-2c5a8d85852f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27685b8e-75cd-51d4-8f57-2c5a8d85852f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436829} :answer-field/id #uuid "afdc93fa-0aca-5068-a46b-19029c20890d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2e5f62-97b7-5f13-893d-335d40132469" :question/math-academy-id "q-317306" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1,\\,4⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,3,\\,-5⟩.$\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,3,\\,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})(-2\\sqrt{2})+(-1)(3)+(4)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-3-20 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126 :question/answer-fiel